mcu_timer: skip cycles on which no timer has a divider edge

TIMER_Clock() steps once per two MCU cycles, which at 12 cycles per
instruction means six iterations for every emulated instruction. Almost all
of them do nothing: TIMER_ClockFrt() and TIMER_ClockTmr() both return
immediately unless timer.cycles sits on their divider edge. Callgrind puts
TIMER_Clock at 29% of all instructions retired in a 32-slot workload, ahead
of PCM_Update.

Compute the distance to the next edge across all four timers and jump there.
Only cycles on which every timer would have returned early are skipped, so
each timer passes through exactly the same sequence of states; nothing
outside the function observes timer.cycles, and the MCU is not running while
we are inside it, so intermediate values are unobservable.

Measured +7% to +13% end to end on x86-64 against a synthetic 32-slot PCM
load. Expected to help more on an in-order Cortex-A53, where retired
instructions cost more.

NOT VALIDATED AGAINST REAL AUDIO. The equivalence argument above is by
construction and the author believes it sound, but the only workload
available when this was written produced pure silence, so an output
comparison proved nothing. Commit 8d6f67a in the core is a previous timer
lookup-table optimisation that silently changed JV880 output, which is
exactly this failure mode. Before trusting this patch, run `sc55d --bench`
with real ROMs on builds with and without it and check that the audio
digests match, on every romset you care about -- JV-880 especially.

--- a/src/backend/mcu_timer.cpp
+++ b/src/backend/mcu_timer.cpp
@@ -387,7 +387,40 @@
 
         TIMER_ClockTmr(timer);
 
-        ++timer.cycles;
+        // TIMER_ClockFrt() and TIMER_ClockTmr() both return immediately unless
+        // timer.cycles sits on their divider edge, and nothing outside this
+        // function observes timer.cycles -- the MCU is not running while we are
+        // in here. So skip straight to the next cycle at which some timer
+        // actually does something rather than visiting every cycle in between.
+        // At 12 MCU cycles per instruction this loop otherwise runs six times
+        // per emulated instruction, almost always to do nothing at all.
+        //
+        // Only cycles on which every timer would have returned early are
+        // skipped, so the sequence of states each timer passes through is
+        // unchanged. Note the tmr step mask of 0, which means "do not step" and
+        // must not be treated as an edge -- mishandling that is what commit
+        // 8d6f67a had to fix.
+        uint64_t step = ~(uint64_t)0;
+
+        for (int i = 0; i < 3; i++)
+        {
+            const uint64_t mask =
+                timer.frt_step_table[timer.frt[i].tcr & (FRT_TCR_CKS0 | FRT_TCR_CKS1)];
+            const uint64_t next = mask + 1 - (timer.cycles & mask);
+            if (next < step)
+                step = next;
+        }
+
+        const uint64_t tmr_mask =
+            timer.tmr_step_table[timer.tmr.tcr & (TMR_TCR_CKS0 | TMR_TCR_CKS1 | TMR_TCR_CKS2)];
+        if (tmr_mask != 0)
+        {
+            const uint64_t next = tmr_mask + 1 - (timer.cycles & tmr_mask);
+            if (next < step)
+                step = next;
+        }
+
+        timer.cycles += step ? step : 1;
     }
 }
 
